      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ Correct — B) DRF Permission / Authorization layer\nYou're getting the distinction now.\nWhy?\nWe already know Amit is authenticated:\n\nJWT valid\n   ↓\nWho is Amit? → Amit ✅\n\nNow we need to check his role:\n\nAmit\n ↓\nOrganizationMember\n ↓\nrole = ADMIN\n ↓\nCan delete organization? → YES\n\n\nWhereas:\nRahul\n ↓\nOrganizationMember\n ↓\nrole = MEMBER\n ↓\nCan delete organization? → NO → 403\n\n\nThat's authorization, so the permission/authorization layer is the appropriate place.\nThe mental model to remember\n| Layer          | Main question                                 |\n| -------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| Authentication | **Who are you?**                              |\n| Permission     | **Are you allowed?**                          |\n| Serializer     | **Is the data valid?**                        |\n| Service        | **How should the business operation happen?** |\n| Model/ORM      | **How is the data stored?**                   |\n\n\nInterview answer\n\n\"Authentication verifies the user's identity, while authorization determines whether that authenticated \nuser has permission to perform an action. In DRF, authorization can be implemented using permission \nclasses, with complex business rules handled in the service/domain layer.\"",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ Correct — B) DRF Permission / Authorization layer\nYou're getting the distinction now.\nWhy?\nWe already know Amit is authenticated:\n\nJWT valid\n   ↓\nWho is Amit? → Amit ✅\n\nNow we need to check his role:\n\nAmit\n ↓\nOrganizationMember\n ↓\nrole = ADMIN\n ↓\nCan delete organization? → YES\n\n\nWhereas:\nRahul\n ↓\nOrganizationMember\n ↓\nrole = MEMBER\n ↓\nCan delete organization? → NO → 403\n\n\nThat's authorization, so the permission/authorization layer is the appropriate place.\nThe mental model to remember\n| Layer          | Main question                                 |\n| -------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| Authentication | **Who are you?**                              |\n| Permission     | **Are you allowed?**                          |\n| Serializer     | **Is the data valid?**                        |\n| Service        | **How should the business operation happen?** |\n| Model/ORM      | **How is the data stored?**                   |\n\n\nInterview answer\n\n\"Authentication verifies the user's identity, while authorization determines whether that authenticated \nuser has permission to perform an action. In DRF, authorization can be implemented using permission \nclasses, with complex business rules handled in the service/domain layer.\"",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "mZtFKagpPukN0upRVtHpX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 29.94632159776961,
      "y": 34792.419890274046,
      "width": 968,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5U",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1595111953,
      "version": 16,
      "versionNonce": 1979635473,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790308364186,
      "created": 1790308343048,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q10 — Let's connect this to our project\nWe have:\n\nOrganization\n    ↓\nTeam\n    ↓\nProject\n    ↓\nTask\n\nA user wants to create a task in Project A.\nThey are a member of the organization, but not a member of the team that owns Project A.\nShould the API allow them to create the task?\n\nA) Yes, organization membership is enough\nB) No, they should be denied because they don't have access to the project's team\nC) Yes, because the JWT is valid\nD) Let the frontend decide",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q10 — Let's connect this to our project\nWe have:\n\nOrganization\n    ↓\nTeam\n    ↓\nProject\n    ↓\nTask\n\nA user wants to create a task in Project A.\nThey are a member of the organization, but not a member of the team that owns Project A.\nShould the API allow them to create the task?\n\nA) Yes, organization membership is enough\nB) No, they should be denied because they don't have access to the project's team\nC) Yes, because the JWT is valid\nD) Let the frontend decide",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "AW4WXKf5opzdM8z6GdDXn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 98.94632159776961,
      "y": 35315.115202774046,
      "width": 1023,
      "height": 1300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 952632497,
      "version": 43,
      "versionNonce": 1843096913,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790308519829,
      "created": 1790308378731,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ Correct — B\n\nExactly.\n\nBeing authenticated or even being an organization member doesn't automatically mean the user \ncan access every team/project.\n\nOur access hierarchy is:\nUser\n ↓\nOrganization membership\n ↓\nTeam membership\n ↓\nProject access\n ↓\nTask access\n\nFor example:\nGoogle\n│\n├── Backend Team\n│    └── Payment Project\n│         └── Task A\n│\n└── Frontend Team\n     └── Dashboard Project\n          └── Task B\n\n\nIf Rahul is:\nGoogle member       ✅\nBackend Team member ❌\n\nthen Rahul shouldn't be able to create a task in Payment Project.\nImportant interview point\n\nDon't just check:\nuser.organization_id == project.organization_id\n\nOur design allows a user to belong to multiple organizations, so we need to check the actual \nmembership relationships:\n\nUser\n ↓\nOrganizationMember\n ↓\nTeamMember\n ↓\nProject\n\nThis is also why we designed OrganizationMember and TeamMember as separate tables earlier.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ Correct — B\n\nExactly.\n\nBeing authenticated or even being an organization member doesn't automatically mean the user \ncan access every team/project.\n\nOur access hierarchy is:\nUser\n ↓\nOrganization membership\n ↓\nTeam membership\n ↓\nProject access\n ↓\nTask access\n\nFor example:\nGoogle\n│\n├── Backend Team\n│    └── Payment Project\n│         └── Task A\n│\n└── Frontend Team\n     └── Dashboard Project\n          └── Task B\n\n\nIf Rahul is:\nGoogle member       ✅\nBackend Team member ❌\n\nthen Rahul shouldn't be able to create a task in Payment Project.\nImportant interview point\n\nDon't just check:\nuser.organization_id == project.organization_id\n\nOur design allows a user to belong to multiple organizations, so we need to check the actual \nmembership relationships:\n\nUser\n ↓\nOrganizationMember\n ↓\nTeamMember\n ↓\nProject\n\nThis is also why we designed OrganizationMember and TeamMember as separate tables earlier.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "00s7yotIISUQapSFaWrU6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 40.95022784776961,
      "y": 36685.419890274046,
      "width": 1012,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5W",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 231293681,
      "version": 15,
      "versionNonce": 2044739697,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790308608009,
      "created": 1790308590384,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q11 — Slightly harder\nNow Rahul is a member of the correct team.\nBut the API request says:\n\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix payment bug\",\n    \"assigned_to\": 50\n}\n\nUser 50 is not a member of that team.\n\nShould the backend allow Rahul to assign the task to user 50?\n\nA) Yes, because user 50 exists\nB) No, the backend should validate that the assignee has access to the relevant team/project\nC) Yes, because Rahul is allowed to create tasks\nD) Let the frontend decide",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q11 — Slightly harder\nNow Rahul is a member of the correct team.\nBut the API request says:\n\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix payment bug\",\n    \"assigned_to\": 50\n}\n\nUser 50 is not a member of that team.\n\nShould the backend allow Rahul to assign the task to user 50?\n\nA) Yes, because user 50 exists\nB) No, the backend should validate that the assignee has access to the relevant team/project\nC) Yes, because Rahul is allowed to create tasks\nD) Let the frontend decide",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "b3cNYM3RxDeRt4u9eP-_G",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 76.95022784776961,
      "y": 37159.662077774046,
      "width": 1056,
      "height": 925,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5X",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 646801937,
      "version": 20,
      "versionNonce": 292980305,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790308692112,
      "created": 1790308624885,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ Correct — B\nExactly. This is an important multi-tenant authorization rule.\nJust because user 50 exists in the database doesn't mean Rahul can assign a task to them.\n\nRahul\n ↓\nTeam Member? ✅\n ↓\nCan create task? ✅\n ↓\nassigned_to = User 50\n ↓\nIs User 50 part of the relevant team/project?\n ↓\n    YES → Allow\n    NO  → Reject\n\nInterview-quality answer\n\n\"When assigning a task, the backend should validate not only that the assignee exists, but also \nthat the assignee has access to the relevant team or project. Authorization checks must be \nenforced server-side rather than relying on the frontend.\"\n\nYou've now covered the important DRF foundation:\nHTTP Methods\n     ↓\nStatus Codes\n     ↓\nSerializers\n     ↓\nValidation\n     ↓\nAuthentication\n     ↓\nPermissions / Authorization\n     ↓\nBusiness Rules",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ Correct — B\nExactly. This is an important multi-tenant authorization rule.\nJust because user 50 exists in the database doesn't mean Rahul can assign a task to them.\n\nRahul\n ↓\nTeam Member? ✅\n ↓\nCan create task? ✅\n ↓\nassigned_to = User 50\n ↓\nIs User 50 part of the relevant team/project?\n ↓\n    YES → Allow\n    NO  → Reject\n\nInterview-quality answer\n\n\"When assigning a task, the backend should validate not only that the assignee exists, but also \nthat the assignee has access to the relevant team or project. Authorization checks must be \nenforced server-side rather than relying on the frontend.\"\n\nYou've now covered the important DRF foundation:\nHTTP Methods\n     ↓\nStatus Codes\n     ↓\nSerializers\n     ↓\nValidation\n     ↓\nAuthentication\n     ↓\nPermissions / Authorization\n     ↓\nBusiness Rules",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Cf2zM4YZjnV6XEJJztJ_d",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -79.04977215223039,
      "y": 38194.544890274046,
      "width": 330,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5Y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 632224753,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 2000410001,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790308866185,
      "created": 1790308864339,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Next topic: APIView vs ViewSet",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Next topic: APIView vs ViewSet",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "0j6KDEzSQhpzTKuQzgYWo",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -41.92867840223039,
      "y": 38231.665984024046,
      "width": 968,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5Z",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1636520063,
      "version": 15,
      "versionNonce": 1908030207,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790308899602,
      "created": 1790308876822,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This is where we'll start writing actual DRF API structure for our Enterprise Workspace.\nBefore we move there, let's do one simple question:\nSuppose we need these APIs:\n\nGET    /projects/\nPOST   /projects/\nGET    /projects/10/\nPATCH  /projects/10/\nDELETE /projects/10/\n\nWould you prefer:\n\nA) Create a completely separate Django view for every endpoint\nB) Use DRF's ViewSet to group related project operations together\nC) Put all APIs in the serializer\nD) Put all APIs in the model",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This is where we'll start writing actual DRF API structure for our Enterprise Workspace.\nBefore we move there, let's do one simple question:\nSuppose we need these APIs:\n\nGET    /projects/\nPOST   /projects/\nGET    /projects/10/\nPATCH  /projects/10/\nDELETE /projects/10/\n\nWould you prefer:\n\nA) Create a completely separate Django view for every endpoint\nB) Use DRF's ViewSet to group related project operations together\nC) Put all APIs in the serializer\nD) Put all APIs in the model",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Rt0SCDZApuOvj5JvAAF1O",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5.313509097769611,
      "y": 38682.150359024046,
      "width": 1001,
      "height": 1300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5a",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1815840607,
      "version": 62,
      "versionNonce": 646939441,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790309110766,
      "created": 1790308939989,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "B) ViewSet\n\nOption A — Separate views\nYou could create:\n    ProjectListView\n    ProjectCreateView\n    ProjectDetailView\n    ProjectUpdateView\n    ProjectDeleteView\nThis works, but you end up with many views for one resource.\n\nOption B — ViewSet\nA ViewSet groups operations for the same resource:\n\nProjectViewSet\n│\n├── list()       → GET /projects/\n├── create()     → POST /projects/\n├── retrieve()   → GET /projects/10/\n├── update()     → PUT /projects/10/\n├── partial_update() → PATCH /projects/10/\n└── destroy()    → DELETE /projects/10/\n\n\nEasy way to remember\n\nAPIView:\n\"I want more control over each endpoint.\"\n\nViewSet:\n\"I want to group CRUD operations for one resource.\"\n\nFor our project, we might have:\nTaskViewSet\nProjectViewSet\nTeamViewSet\nDocumentViewSet\n\nEach groups operations related to that resource.\n\n\nOne important nuance\nDon't memorize:\n\"Always use ViewSet.\"\nAPIView can be preferable for unusual/custom endpoints where CRUD doesn't fit naturally.\n\nFor example:\nPOST /projects/10/archive/\nPOST /tasks/10/complete/\nPOST /documents/10/summarize/\n\nThese aren't simple CRUD operations, so an APIView or custom ViewSet action can make sense.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "B) ViewSet\n\nOption A — Separate views\nYou could create:\n    ProjectListView\n    ProjectCreateView\n    ProjectDetailView\n    ProjectUpdateView\n    ProjectDeleteView\nThis works, but you end up with many views for one resource.\n\nOption B — ViewSet\nA ViewSet groups operations for the same resource:\n\nProjectViewSet\n│\n├── list()       → GET /projects/\n├── create()     → POST /projects/\n├── retrieve()   → GET /projects/10/\n├── update()     → PUT /projects/10/\n├── partial_update() → PATCH /projects/10/\n└── destroy()    → DELETE /projects/10/\n\n\nEasy way to remember\n\nAPIView:\n\"I want more control over each endpoint.\"\n\nViewSet:\n\"I want to group CRUD operations for one resource.\"\n\nFor our project, we might have:\nTaskViewSet\nProjectViewSet\nTeamViewSet\nDocumentViewSet\n\nEach groups operations related to that resource.\n\n\nOne important nuance\nDon't memorize:\n\"Always use ViewSet.\"\nAPIView can be preferable for unusual/custom endpoints where CRUD doesn't fit naturally.\n\nFor example:\nPOST /projects/10/archive/\nPOST /tasks/10/complete/\nPOST /documents/10/summarize/\n\nThese aren't simple CRUD operations, so an APIView or custom ViewSet action can make sense.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Z83pbD1zzG6fnVoVkJA4z",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -71.04977215223039,
      "y": 40051.665984024046,
      "width": 726,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5b",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1058710737,
      "version": 17,
      "versionNonce": 1178980543,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790309249206,
      "created": 1790309212160,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Next question\nImagine:\nGET /projects/\n\nWe want:\nReturn all projects belonging to the current user's organizations.\nWhere should we primarily put the database filtering logic?\n\nA) Serializer\nB) View/ViewSet or service layer\nC) Frontend\nD) Model field definition\n\nWhat's your answer?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Next question\nImagine:\nGET /projects/\n\nWe want:\nReturn all projects belonging to the current user's organizations.\nWhere should we primarily put the database filtering logic?\n\nA) Serializer\nB) View/ViewSet or service layer\nC) Frontend\nD) Model field definition\n\nWhat's your answer?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Kp5lPIeZeNlIFDmYg0Bv6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 131.9502278477696,
      "y": 40096.544890274046,
      "width": 11,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5c",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 895135423,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 2014193777,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790309238562,
      "created": 1790309238027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "tD1m9QgZz_hS_1mtwfOPd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -22.04977215223039,
      "y": 40436.787077774046,
      "width": 1067,
      "height": 925,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5d",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 723169361,
      "version": 30,
      "versionNonce": 1922882431,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790309393596,
      "created": 1790309304395,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ Correct — B\nExactly.\n\nThe serializer should mainly transform and validate data. Filtering which projects the current\n user is allowed to see is access/business logic, so it belongs in the View/ViewSet or, for more \ncomplex logic, a service layer.\n\nFor example, conceptually:\ndef get_queryset(self):\n    user = self.request.user\n\n    return Project.objects.filter(\n        team__members__user=user\n    )\n\nThe important part is that we don't do: Project.objects.all()\nbecause that could expose projects the user shouldn't see.\n\nRequest flow\nGET /projects/\n      ↓\nAuthentication\n      ↓\nPermission\n      ↓\nViewSet\n      ↓\nFilter queryset for current user\n      ↓\nSerializer\n      ↓\nJSON response\n\nInterview point\n\nAuthentication identifies the user, permissions determine whether they can access something, \nand the queryset determines which records they can actually retrieve.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ Correct — B\nExactly.\n\nThe serializer should mainly transform and validate data. Filtering which projects the current\n user is allowed to see is access/business logic, so it belongs in the View/ViewSet or, for more \ncomplex logic, a service layer.\n\nFor example, conceptually:\ndef get_queryset(self):\n    user = self.request.user\n\n    return Project.objects.filter(\n        team__members__user=user\n    )\n\nThe important part is that we don't do: Project.objects.all()\nbecause that could expose projects the user shouldn't see.\n\nRequest flow\nGET /projects/\n      ↓\nAuthentication\n      ↓\nPermission\n      ↓\nViewSet\n      ↓\nFilter queryset for current user\n      ↓\nSerializer\n      ↓\nJSON response\n\nInterview point\n\nAuthentication identifies the user, permissions determine whether they can access something, \nand the queryset determines which records they can actually retrieve.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "DG3IgyDwRfJCM8_PXEyvI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -112.93258465223039,
      "y": 41408.544890274046,
      "width": 1045,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5e",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2129393631,
      "version": 12,
      "versionNonce": 356009471,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790309449953,
      "created": 1790309431879,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q12 — APIView vs ViewSet\nWhen would an APIView be a better choice than a normal CRUD ViewSet?\nFor example: POST /documents/15/summarize/\n\nThis doesn't create/update/delete the document itself. It performs a custom business operation.\n\nWhich is more appropriate?\n\nA) APIView or a custom ViewSet action\nB) Serializer only\nC) Django model only\nD) Frontend",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q12 — APIView vs ViewSet\nWhen would an APIView be a better choice than a normal CRUD ViewSet?\nFor example: POST /documents/15/summarize/\n\nThis doesn't create/update/delete the document itself. It performs a custom business operation.\n\nWhich is more appropriate?\n\nA) APIView or a custom ViewSet action\nB) Serializer only\nC) Django model only\nD) Frontend",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "EwSnxozlusVaeNwUhLzi0",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -57.56539715223039,
      "y": 41741.665984024046,
      "width": 902,
      "height": 1075,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5f",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1526507281,
      "version": 34,
      "versionNonce": 1925803985,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790309582946,
      "created": 1790309517149,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ Correct — A\nExactly.\nPOST /documents/15/summarize/ is a custom operation, not normal CRUD.\n\nCRUD\n├── GET       → retrieve\n├── POST      → create\n├── PATCH     → update\n└── DELETE    → delete\n\nBut:\nPOST /documents/15/summarize/\n                ↓\n        Custom business action\n\nFor this, you can use:\n\nAPIView — especially when the endpoint has its own workflow.\nCustom ViewSet action — if you already have a DocumentViewSet.\n\nFor example:\n@action(detail=True, methods=[\"post\"])\ndef summarize(self, request, pk=None):\n    ...\n\nYour DRF mental model so far\nRequest\n   ↓\nAuthentication\n   ↓\nPermission\n   ↓\nView / ViewSet\n   ↓\nSerializer\n   ↓\nService / Business Logic\n   ↓\nORM\n   ↓\nDatabase\n\nYou're now at a good point to move from conceptual DRF into actual implementation.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ Correct — A\nExactly.\nPOST /documents/15/summarize/ is a custom operation, not normal CRUD.\n\nCRUD\n├── GET       → retrieve\n├── POST      → create\n├── PATCH     → update\n└── DELETE    → delete\n\nBut:\nPOST /documents/15/summarize/\n                ↓\n        Custom business action\n\nFor this, you can use:\n\nAPIView — especially when the endpoint has its own workflow.\nCustom ViewSet action — if you already have a DocumentViewSet.\n\nFor example:\n@action(detail=True, methods=[\"post\"])\ndef summarize(self, request, pk=None):\n    ...\n\nYour DRF mental model so far\nRequest\n   ↓\nAuthentication\n   ↓\nPermission\n   ↓\nView / ViewSet\n   ↓\nSerializer\n   ↓\nService / Business Logic\n   ↓\nORM\n   ↓\nDatabase\n\nYou're now at a good point to move from conceptual DRF into actual implementation.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "1bNN39_VIHCvVr0j8Jxoz",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -150.0458659022304,
      "y": 42912.415984024046,
      "width": 792,
      "height": 375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5g",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1425964881,
      "version": 58,
      "versionNonce": 123376668,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790571681012,
      "created": 1790309596499,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Next: Service Layer\nWe'll answer an important question:\nWhy shouldn't we put all our business logic directly inside the ViewSet?\nThen we'll build something like:\n\nPOST /projects/10/tasks/\n          ↓\nTaskViewSet\n          ↓\nTaskService.create_task(...)\n          ↓\nValidation + business rules\n          ↓\nTask.objects.create(...)\nThis is the next concept we'll continue with.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Next: Service Layer\nWe'll answer an important question:\nWhy shouldn't we put all our business logic directly inside the ViewSet?\nThen we'll build something like:\n\nPOST /projects/10/tasks/\n          ↓\nTaskViewSet\n          ↓\nTaskService.create_task(...)\n          ↓\nValidation + business rules\n          ↓\nTask.objects.create(...)\nThis is the next concept we'll continue with.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "-Aln6sYXL7Bu8foExvjVk",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -145.9403971522304,
      "y": 43349.021452774046,
      "width": 1089,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5h",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1435608604,
      "version": 11,
      "versionNonce": 1537118628,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790571709327,
      "created": 1790571682850,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. First, understand the problem\nImagine our Enterprise Workspace has this API:\n\nPOST /projects/10/tasks/\n\nThe user wants to create a task.\nThe backend needs to:\n1. Check whether the user can create tasks in Project 10.\n2. Validate the task data.\n3. Check whether the assigned user belongs to the project’s team.\n4. Create the task in PostgreSQL.\n5. Record an activity log.\n6. Send a notification to the assignee.\nQuestion: Should we write all six steps inside the APIView?\nWe technically can. But as the application grows, the view becomes large and difficult to maintain.\nThat is where the Service Layer helps.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. First, understand the problem\nImagine our Enterprise Workspace has this API:\n\nPOST /projects/10/tasks/\n\nThe user wants to create a task.\nThe backend needs to:\n1. Check whether the user can create tasks in Project 10.\n2. Validate the task data.\n3. Check whether the assigned user belongs to the project’s team.\n4. Create the task in PostgreSQL.\n5. Record an activity log.\n6. Send a notification to the assignee.\nQuestion: Should we write all six steps inside the APIView?\nWe technically can. But as the application grows, the view becomes large and difficult to maintain.\nThat is where the Service Layer helps.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "JHtLQ1hF03WhJ10vwuE9B",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -142.8153971522304,
      "y": 43810.900359024046,
      "width": 297,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5i",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 667328420,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 1121322660,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790571772244,
      "created": 1790571771768,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. What is a Service Layer?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. What is a Service Layer?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "k1caJBDKcEvm-qvu0yafS",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -106.69430340223039,
      "y": 43850.900359024046,
      "width": 1034,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1582185244,
      "version": 12,
      "versionNonce": 1602055196,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790571899817,
      "created": 1790571777234,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A service layer is a place where we write and organize our application's business operations.\nThink of a restaurant:\n\nWaiter — APIView\nTakes the customer's request\n\nChef — Service Layer\nCoordinates how the order is prepared\n\nKitchen resources — ORM / Database\nStore and retrieve the required data\n\nThe waiter doesn't cook the food. Similarly, the APIView shouldn't need to contain the entire \nbusiness workflow.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A service layer is a place where we write and organize our application's business operations.\nThink of a restaurant:\n\nWaiter — APIView\nTakes the customer's request\n\nChef — Service Layer\nCoordinates how the order is prepared\n\nKitchen resources — ORM / Database\nStore and retrieve the required data\n\nThe waiter doesn't cook the food. Similarly, the APIView shouldn't need to contain the entire \nbusiness workflow.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "fyL6UZp7qe55iaADlxP3z",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -131.5380534022304,
      "y": 44251.040984024046,
      "width": 363,
      "height": 50,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5k",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 610603420,
      "version": 60,
      "versionNonce": 1698351268,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790571966642,
      "created": 1790571946082,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "3. Without a Service Layer\nConsider this simplified APIView:",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "3. Without a Service Layer\nConsider this simplified APIView:",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "CcLbOt-jFkRH_lrH721-i",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -99.81539715223039,
      "y": 44319.900359024046,
      "width": 1023,
      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5l",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 53623068,
      "version": 7,
      "versionNonce": 1962286748,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790572017396,
      "created": 1790571956515,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "class TaskCreateAPIView(APIView):\n\n    def post(self, request, project_id):\n        serializer = TaskSerializer(data=request.data)\n        serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n\n        project = Project.objects.get(id=project_id)\n\n        task = Task.objects.create(\n            project=project,\n            title=serializer.validated_data[\"title\"],\n            created_by=request.user,\n        )\n\n        ActivityLog.objects.create(\n            task=task,\n            action=\"TASK_CREATED\",\n            user=request.user,\n        )\n\n        # Send notification\n        # Update project statistics\n\n        return Response(\n            {\"id\": task.id},\n            status=201,\n        )\n\nAs you can see, the APIView is handling HTTP, database operations, and the business workflow.\nImagine adding ten more rules. The method will keep growing.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "class TaskCreateAPIView(APIView):\n\n    def post(self, request, project_id):\n        serializer = TaskSerializer(data=request.data)\n        serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n\n        project = Project.objects.get(id=project_id)\n\n        task = Task.objects.create(\n            project=project,\n            title=serializer.validated_data[\"title\"],\n            created_by=request.user,\n        )\n\n        ActivityLog.objects.create(\n            task=task,\n            action=\"TASK_CREATED\",\n            user=request.user,\n        )\n\n        # Send notification\n        # Update project statistics\n\n        return Response(\n            {\"id\": task.id},\n            status=201,\n        )\n\nAs you can see, the APIView is handling HTTP, database operations, and the business workflow.\nImagine adding ten more rules. The method will keep growing.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "RZVNtRLrwoh-Q3CGqdO5w",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -147.8153971522304,
      "y": 45144.900359024046,
      "width": 363,
      "height": 75,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5m",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 499962908,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 218986908,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790572026777,
      "created": 1790572024876,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "4. With a Service Layer\nWe separate the responsibilities.\nFile structure",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "4. With a Service Layer\nWe separate the responsibilities.\nFile structure",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "-FFFdBT6wjpKl2c1nibyJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -97.81539715223039,
      "y": 45238.779265274046,
      "width": 1133,
      "height": 1200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5n",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2128738332,
      "version": 21,
      "versionNonce": 2113645980,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790572296452,
      "created": 1790572066450,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "apps/\n└── tasks/\n    ├── models.py\n    ├── serializers.py\n    ├── views.py\n    ├── services.py\n    ├── permissions.py\n    └── urls.py\n\nFor now, we'll keep services.py in the same Django app. We don't need to create a separate service app.\nservices.py\n\nfrom django.db import transaction\n\nfrom .models import Task, ActivityLog\n\n\nclass TaskService:\n\n    @staticmethod\n    @transaction.atomic\n    def create_task(*, user, project, validated_data):\n\n        task = Task.objects.create(\n            project=project,\n            created_by=user,\n            **validated_data,\n        )\n\n        ActivityLog.objects.create(\n            task=task,\n            action=\"TASK_CREATED\",\n            user=user,\n        )\n\n        return task\n\nLet's understand the important parts.\n- TaskService: groups operations related to tasks.\n- create_task(): contains the workflow for creating a task.\n- user: identifies who initiated the operation.\n- project: the project in which the task is being created.\n- validated_data: the data already validated by the serializer.\n- transaction.atomic: makes the database writes in this transaction succeed or roll back together \nif an error occurs.\n\nFor example, if creating the task succeeds but creating its activity log fails, the transaction rolls \nback the task creation too. This example assumes both models use the same transactional database.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "apps/\n└── tasks/\n    ├── models.py\n    ├── serializers.py\n    ├── views.py\n    ├── services.py\n    ├── permissions.py\n    └── urls.py\n\nFor now, we'll keep services.py in the same Django app. We don't need to create a separate service app.\nservices.py\n\nfrom django.db import transaction\n\nfrom .models import Task, ActivityLog\n\n\nclass TaskService:\n\n    @staticmethod\n    @transaction.atomic\n    def create_task(*, user, project, validated_data):\n\n        task = Task.objects.create(\n            project=project,\n            created_by=user,\n            **validated_data,\n        )\n\n        ActivityLog.objects.create(\n            task=task,\n            action=\"TASK_CREATED\",\n            user=user,\n        )\n\n        return task\n\nLet's understand the important parts.\n- TaskService: groups operations related to tasks.\n- create_task(): contains the workflow for creating a task.\n- user: identifies who initiated the operation.\n- project: the project in which the task is being created.\n- validated_data: the data already validated by the serializer.\n- transaction.atomic: makes the database writes in this transaction succeed or roll back together \nif an error occurs.\n\nFor example, if creating the task succeeds but creating its activity log fails, the transaction rolls \nback the task creation too. This example assumes both models use the same transactional database.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "hFtMy5UcrUpTIPENWAX0B",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -146.8153971522304,
      "y": 46515.900359024046,
      "width": 396,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5o",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 583896228,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 211263268,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790572311646,
      "created": 1790572309068,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "5. Calling the service from views.py",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "5. Calling the service from views.py",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "4_u72egiHu7mcElsFE5Ks",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -70.69430340223039,
      "y": 46561.021452774046,
      "width": 1144,
      "height": 1150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5p",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 453831972,
      "version": 13,
      "versionNonce": 24844316,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790572398997,
      "created": 1790572345169,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "from rest_framework.views import APIView\nfrom rest_framework.response import Response\nfrom rest_framework import status\n\nfrom .serializers import TaskSerializer\nfrom .services import TaskService\n\n\nclass TaskCreateAPIView(APIView):\n\n    def post(self, request, project_id):\n        serializer = TaskSerializer(data=request.data)\n        serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n\n        project = get_object_or_404(\n            Project,\n            id=project_id,\n        )\n\n        task = TaskService.create_task(\n            user=request.user,\n            project=project,\n            validated_data=serializer.validated_data,\n        )\n\n        return Response(\n            TaskSerializer(task).data,\n            status=status.HTTP_201_CREATED,\n        )\n\n\nHere, get_object_or_404 needs to be imported from django.shortcuts, and Project from your models. \nIn production, the project lookup must also be scoped to projects the current user is allowed to access.\nThe key point is that the APIView now delegates the creation workflow:\n\nRequest\n   ↓\nAPIView\n   ↓\nSerializer validation\n   ↓\nTaskService.create_task()\n   ↓\nCreate Task + Activity Log\n   ↓\nResponse",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "from rest_framework.views import APIView\nfrom rest_framework.response import Response\nfrom rest_framework import status\n\nfrom .serializers import TaskSerializer\nfrom .services import TaskService\n\n\nclass TaskCreateAPIView(APIView):\n\n    def post(self, request, project_id):\n        serializer = TaskSerializer(data=request.data)\n        serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n\n        project = get_object_or_404(\n            Project,\n            id=project_id,\n        )\n\n        task = TaskService.create_task(\n            user=request.user,\n            project=project,\n            validated_data=serializer.validated_data,\n        )\n\n        return Response(\n            TaskSerializer(task).data,\n            status=status.HTTP_201_CREATED,\n        )\n\n\nHere, get_object_or_404 needs to be imported from django.shortcuts, and Project from your models. \nIn production, the project lookup must also be scoped to projects the current user is allowed to access.\nThe key point is that the APIView now delegates the creation workflow:\n\nRequest\n   ↓\nAPIView\n   ↓\nSerializer validation\n   ↓\nTaskService.create_task()\n   ↓\nCreate Task + Activity Log\n   ↓\nResponse",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "us8XVpr4D6g4S4wM8mM5k",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -126.81539715223039,
      "y": 47774.900359024046,
      "width": 242,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1588739484,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 1592349468,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790572410233,
      "created": 1790572408536,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "6. Why is this useful?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "6. Why is this useful?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "mCFRs2DS__Ymm_Mk3LKJ3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -78.81539715223039,
      "y": 47808.900359024046,
      "width": 924,
      "height": 150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5r",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 297668388,
      "version": 6,
      "versionNonce": 1495378724,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790572446594,
      "created": 1790572436686,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Suppose we later want to create tasks from two places:\n- A REST API request\n- A scheduled Celery job\nWithout a service layer, you might duplicate the task-creation logic in both places.\nWith a service layer, both can call:\nTaskService.create_task(...)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Suppose we later want to create tasks from two places:\n- A REST API request\n- A scheduled Celery job\nWithout a service layer, you might duplicate the task-creation logic in both places.\nWith a service layer, both can call:\nTaskService.create_task(...)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "q3FOkXxxWYxlVz_b1wAa4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -132.9403971522304,
      "y": 48042.775359024046,
      "width": 704,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5s",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1593986468,
      "version": 11,
      "versionNonce": 1809876388,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790572606651,
      "created": 1790572592204,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q1 — Your turn\nSuppose we have this API:\n\nclass TaskCreateAPIView(APIView):\n\n    def post(self, request):\n        # Validate data\n        # Check project access\n        # Create task\n        # Create activity log\n        # Send notification\n        # Return response\n        ...\n\nWe want to move the task-creation workflow into TaskService.\nWhich responsibility should remain primarily inside the APIView?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q1 — Your turn\nSuppose we have this API:\n\nclass TaskCreateAPIView(APIView):\n\n    def post(self, request):\n        # Validate data\n        # Check project access\n        # Create task\n        # Create activity log\n        # Send notification\n        # Return response\n        ...\n\nWe want to move the task-creation workflow into TaskService.\nWhich responsibility should remain primarily inside the APIView?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "T8rYwMPfLwYHBf0WyMKMZ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -76.93649090223039,
      "y": 48473.900359024046,
      "width": 913,
      "height": 225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5t",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 176240676,
      "version": 13,
      "versionNonce": 62285724,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790572674688,
      "created": 1790572643922,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A. Creating the task and activity log in the database\nB. Handling the HTTP request and returning the HTTP response\nC. Implementing every business rule\nD. Coordinating all notification and analytics workflows\n\nAnswer: B\nThe APIView handles HTTP concerns: receiving the request, invoking validation and \napplication logic, and returning the appropriate response. The service coordinates \nthe business workflow.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A. Creating the task and activity log in the database\nB. Handling the HTTP request and returning the HTTP response\nC. Implementing every business rule\nD. Coordinating all notification and analytics workflows\n\nAnswer: B\nThe APIView handles HTTP concerns: receiving the request, invoking validation and \napplication logic, and returning the appropriate response. The service coordinates \nthe business workflow.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "DLLqxf1GlGZOdm9YhrJez",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -179.9403971522304,
      "y": 48767.775359024046,
      "width": 1001,
      "height": 550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5u",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 849722652,
      "version": 19,
      "versionNonce": 52004516,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790572852511,
      "created": 1790572739654,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Next, Amit, we'll continue Day 4 — Service Layer, moving from understanding the concept to \nimplementing it properly in our Enterprise Workspace using APIView.\nHere's the plan:\n\n1. Service Layer fundamentals -> done\nWhy we separate business logic from APIViews. Covered.\n\n\n2. Build a proper Task API\nAPIView → Serializer → Permission → Service → ORM, with actual Django code.\n\n\n3. Transactions and error handling\nUse transaction.atomic, handle missing projects, and prevent partial database updates.\n\n\n4. Testing the service\nTest business logic independently of HTTP requests.\n\n\n5. Practice and interview questions\nApply the concepts and explain your design decisions.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Next, Amit, we'll continue Day 4 — Service Layer, moving from understanding the concept to \nimplementing it properly in our Enterprise Workspace using APIView.\nHere's the plan:\n\n1. Service Layer fundamentals -> done\nWhy we separate business logic from APIViews. Covered.\n\n\n2. Build a proper Task API\nAPIView → Serializer → Permission → Service → ORM, with actual Django code.\n\n\n3. Transactions and error handling\nUse transaction.atomic, handle missing projects, and prevent partial database updates.\n\n\n4. Testing the service\nTest business logic independently of HTTP requests.\n\n\n5. Practice and interview questions\nApply the concepts and explain your design decisions.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "K_5hWjFJF9uHo3BhawvJi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -183.5810221522304,
      "y": 49404.779265274046,
      "width": 968,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5v",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 619567396,
      "version": 17,
      "versionNonce": 1038724004,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790573155089,
      "created": 1790572867903,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Let's continue with Step 2\nWe'll build POST /projects/{project_id}/tasks/ using APIView and a separate TaskService.\nThe first thing to understand is where the project lookup belongs.\n\nSuppose the request is: POST /projects/10/tasks/\n\nThe backend must fetch Project 10 and ensure the current user is allowed to access it.\nShould we:\n- A. Fetch the project in the APIView, then pass the project object to the service.\n- B. Pass only project_id to the service and let the service fetch the project.\n- C. Fetch the project inside the serializer.\nFor our learning example, which approach would you choose, and why?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Let's continue with Step 2\nWe'll build POST /projects/{project_id}/tasks/ using APIView and a separate TaskService.\nThe first thing to understand is where the project lookup belongs.\n\nSuppose the request is: POST /projects/10/tasks/\n\nThe backend must fetch Project 10 and ensure the current user is allowed to access it.\nShould we:\n- A. Fetch the project in the APIView, then pass the project object to the service.\n- B. Pass only project_id to the service and let the service fetch the project.\n- C. Fetch the project inside the serializer.\nFor our learning example, which approach would you choose, and why?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Q5rnqyoEN1M9twGSmwnXn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2.184602847769611,
      "y": 49525.900359024046,
      "width": 11,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5w",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1566698524,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 1191303844,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790572893818,
      "created": 1790572893518,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "jq_cFI6eC-9V2AYjQI7Tp",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -152.1903971522304,
      "y": 49741.021452774046,
      "width": 1430,
      "height": 1350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5x",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 448128292,
      "version": 24,
      "versionNonce": 456337956,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790574009140,
      "created": 1790573592861,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ B is a valid choice — and it's a good design when the service owns the complete task-creation \nworkflow.\nLet's understand why.\nWhat happens with Option B?\nThe APIView passes the project_id to the service:\n\nclass TaskCreateAPIView(APIView):\n\n    def post(self, request, project_id):\n        serializer = TaskSerializer(data=request.data)\n        serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n\n        task = TaskService.create_task(\n            user=request.user,\n            project_id=project_id,\n            validated_data=serializer.validated_data,\n        )\n\n        return Response(\n            TaskSerializer(task).data,\n            status=201,\n        )\n\nThe service fetches the project and creates the task:\n\nclass TaskService:\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def create_task(*, user, project_id, validated_data):\n        project = Project.objects.get(id=project_id)\n\n        # Business rules go here\n\n        return Task.objects.create(\n            project=project,\n            created_by=user,\n            **validated_data,\n        )\n\n\nBut there's an important distinction\nFetching a project and checking whether the user is allowed to access it are two different things.\n- Fetching: Does Project 10 exist?\n- Authorization: Is this user allowed to create tasks in Project 10?\nWe must enforce authorization regardless of where the project is fetched. A service that accepts a \nproject_id must not blindly trust it.\nAlso, in a real application, we might use a permission class to reject unauthorized requests before the \nservice runs. The service can still enforce critical business rules.\nWhen would A be useful?\nIf the APIView has already fetched and authorized the project, passing the project object to the service avoids fetching it again.\nRule of thumb:\n- Pass project_id when the service should own the lookup.\n- Pass project when the caller has already fetched it and the service can safely use that object.\nNeither approach is universally correct.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ B is a valid choice — and it's a good design when the service owns the complete task-creation \nworkflow.\nLet's understand why.\nWhat happens with Option B?\nThe APIView passes the project_id to the service:\n\nclass TaskCreateAPIView(APIView):\n\n    def post(self, request, project_id):\n        serializer = TaskSerializer(data=request.data)\n        serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n\n        task = TaskService.create_task(\n            user=request.user,\n            project_id=project_id,\n            validated_data=serializer.validated_data,\n        )\n\n        return Response(\n            TaskSerializer(task).data,\n            status=201,\n        )\n\nThe service fetches the project and creates the task:\n\nclass TaskService:\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def create_task(*, user, project_id, validated_data):\n        project = Project.objects.get(id=project_id)\n\n        # Business rules go here\n\n        return Task.objects.create(\n            project=project,\n            created_by=user,\n            **validated_data,\n        )\n\n\nBut there's an important distinction\nFetching a project and checking whether the user is allowed to access it are two different things.\n- Fetching: Does Project 10 exist?\n- Authorization: Is this user allowed to create tasks in Project 10?\nWe must enforce authorization regardless of where the project is fetched. A service that accepts a \nproject_id must not blindly trust it.\nAlso, in a real application, we might use a permission class to reject unauthorized requests before the \nservice runs. The service can still enforce critical business rules.\nWhen would A be useful?\nIf the APIView has already fetched and authorized the project, passing the project object to the service avoids fetching it again.\nRule of thumb:\n- Pass project_id when the service should own the lookup.\n- Pass project when the caller has already fetched it and the service can safely use that object.\nNeither approach is universally correct.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "a8KngayRagt-2vDEk4e8V",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -204.8153971522304,
      "y": 51150.787077774046,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 904993611,
      "version": 8,
      "versionNonce": 1902401355,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790747690701,
      "created": 1790747650274,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q2 — A database safety question\nOur service performs two operations:\n1. Create a task.\n2. Create an activity log.\nSuppose task creation succeeds, but activity-log creation fails.\nWe don't want the database to contain the task without its corresponding\nactivity log.\nWhich Django feature should we use to make both database operations\nsucceed or fail together?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q2 — A database safety question\nOur service performs two operations:\n1. Create a task.\n2. Create an activity log.\nSuppose task creation succeeds, but activity-log creation fails.\nWe don't want the database to contain the task without its corresponding activity log.\nWhich Django feature should we use to make both database operations succeed or fail together?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "EiiTQKxor3SBII8G5NhIQ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -175.9833659022304,
      "y": 51390.462859024046,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 1250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5z",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 932168549,
      "version": 97,
      "versionNonce": 1788177227,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790747906244,
      "created": 1790747668539,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A. transaction.atomic()\nB. serializer.is_valid()\nC. APIView\nD. select_related()\n\nAnswer: A — transaction.atomic()\nIt wraps the database operations in a transaction. If an exception\ncauses the transaction to roll back, both writes are undone, provided\nthey're part of the same database transaction.\n\nIt ensures the two database operations behave as one unit:\nCreate Task\n    ↓\nCreate Activity Log\n    ↓\nBoth succeed → COMMIT ✅\n\nIf either fails\n    ↓\nROLLBACK ❌\n\nFor example:\n\nfrom django.db import transaction\n\nclass TaskService:\n\n    @staticmethod\n    @transaction.atomic\n    def create_task(*, user, project_id, validated_data):\n\n        task = Task.objects.create(\n            project_id=project_id,\n            created_by=user,\n            **validated_data\n        )\n\n        ActivityLog.objects.create(\n            task=task,\n            user=user,\n            action=\"TASK_CREATED\"\n        )\n\n        return task\n\n\nInterview answer\n\"transaction.atomic() groups multiple database operations into a single\ntransaction. If an exception occurs, the transaction is rolled back so\nwe don't leave partially completed data.\"",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A. transaction.atomic()\nB. serializer.is_valid()\nC. APIView\nD. select_related()\n\nAnswer: A — transaction.atomic()\nIt wraps the database operations in a transaction. If an exception causes the transaction to roll back, both writes are undone, provided they're part of the same database transaction.\n\nIt ensures the two database operations behave as one unit:\nCreate Task\n    ↓\nCreate Activity Log\n    ↓\nBoth succeed → COMMIT ✅\n\nIf either fails\n    ↓\nROLLBACK ❌\n\nFor example:\n\nfrom django.db import transaction\n\nclass TaskService:\n\n    @staticmethod\n    @transaction.atomic\n    def create_task(*, user, project_id, validated_data):\n\n        task = Task.objects.create(\n            project_id=project_id,\n            created_by=user,\n            **validated_data\n        )\n\n        ActivityLog.objects.create(\n            task=task,\n            user=user,\n            action=\"TASK_CREATED\"\n        )\n\n        return task\n\n\nInterview answer\n\"transaction.atomic() groups multiple database operations into a single transaction. If an exception occurs, the transaction is rolled back so we don't leave partially completed data.\"",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "ixl0ryAcEHTsan4C1Tfjo",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -236.8114909022304,
      "y": 52715.665984024046,
      "width": 781,
      "height": 50,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b60",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 16812843,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 1697462027,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790747916717,
      "created": 1790747915122,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Next: Custom Exceptions\nSuppose our service finds that the user isn't allowed to create a task:",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Next: Custom Exceptions\nSuppose our service finds that the user isn't allowed to create a task:",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Xv9Yss6cR7b4WXML4CU5t",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -185.7919596522304,
      "y": 52780.931609024046,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b61",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1189349701,
      "version": 27,
      "versionNonce": 286546443,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790747973698,
      "created": 1790747926200,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "if not user_can_create_task:\n    ...\n\nWe don't want the service to return an HTTP Response, because the\nservice shouldn't know about HTTP.\nInstead, we can raise an application exception:\n\nraise PermissionError(\"You cannot create a task in this project.\")\n\nThen the API layer can translate that into: 403 Forbidden\n\nThis keeps the separation clean:\n\nService\n  ↓\nRaises business/application error\n  ↓\nAPIView\n  ↓\nHTTP response",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "if not user_can_create_task:\n    ...\n\nWe don't want the service to return an HTTP Response, because the service shouldn't know about HTTP.\nInstead, we can raise an application exception:\n\nraise PermissionError(\"You cannot create a task in this project.\")\n\nThen the API layer can translate that into: 403 Forbidden\n\nThis keeps the separation clean:\n\nService\n  ↓\nRaises business/application error\n  ↓\nAPIView\n  ↓\nHTTP response",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "gwZcyJnZiygc9WG0W9BTS",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -287.4443034022304,
      "y": 52843.728484024046,
      "width": 517,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b62",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 839246731,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 54836427,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790747946522,
      "created": 1790747945598,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Instead, we can raise an application exception:",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Instead, we can raise an application exception:",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "2kBImbn8o_N-0r8caKdZS",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -252.2099284022304,
      "y": 53354.900359024046,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b63",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1126081957,
      "version": 14,
      "versionNonce": 294540549,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790749595022,
      "created": 1790749547286,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q — Your turn\nWhy shouldn't TaskService directly return this?\nreturn Response(\n    {\"error\": \"Forbidden\"},\n    status=403\n)\n\nA) Because services should not depend directly on HTTP/DRF response\nobjects\nB) Because Response cannot return JSON\nC) Because services cannot raise errors\nD) Because Django doesn't support Response\nWhat do you think?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q — Your turn\nWhy shouldn't TaskService directly return this?\nreturn Response(\n    {\"error\": \"Forbidden\"},\n    status=403\n)\n\nA) Because services should not depend directly on HTTP/DRF response objects\nB) Because Response cannot return JSON\nC) Because services cannot raise errors\nD) Because Django doesn't support Response\nWhat do you think?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Yskz0xAbs_tTzSApNloKh",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -201.8544596522304,
      "y": 53703.771452774046,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 2350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b64",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1409510597,
      "version": 80,
      "versionNonce": 1520108491,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790749953345,
      "created": 1790749574470,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ Correct — A\nExactly.\nThe Service Layer should not be coupled to HTTP/DRF.\nThink about the separation\n\n                 HTTP world\n                    ↓\n               APIView\n                    ↓\n              Serializer\n                    ↓\n              Service Layer\n                    ↓\n              Database\n\nThe service should be usable even when there is no HTTP request.\nFor example, later our Celery worker might call:\n\nTaskService.create_task(...)\n\nThere is no request and no HTTP Response there.\nSo this is bad inside a service:\n\nreturn Response(..., status=403)\nInstead: raise PermissionError(\"User cannot create task\")\n\nThen the API layer decides how to represent that error over HTTP.\n\nBut let's improve PermissionError\nPython's built-in PermissionError works, but in a real Django\napplication we often create custom application exceptions.\nFor example:\n\nclass ProjectAccessDenied(Exception):\n    pass\n\nThen:\nif not user_can_access_project:\n    raise ProjectAccessDenied()\n\nThe API layer can translate it to:\nProjectAccessDenied\n        ↓\n403 Forbidden\n\nThis gives us a clean separation:\n\nService\n  ↓\n\"Business operation failed\"\n  ↓\nException\n  ↓\nAPIView / Exception Handler\n  ↓\nHTTP 403\n\nNow let's build the complete flow\nFor our task creation API:\n\nPOST /projects/10/tasks/\nThe flow becomes:\nClient\n  ↓\nAPIView\n  ↓\nAuthentication\n  ↓\nPermission\n  ↓\nSerializer\n  ↓\nTaskService\n  ↓\nBusiness Rules\n  ↓\ntransaction.atomic()\n  ↓\nTask + ActivityLog\n  ↓\nResponse\n\nAnd the responsibilities are:\n\n\n| Layer | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| APIView | HTTP request/response |\n| Authentication | Identify user |\n| Permission | Basic access control |\n| Serializer | Validate input |\n| Service | Business workflow |\n| Transaction | Keep related DB changes atomic |\n| ORM | Database operations |",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ Correct — A\nExactly.\nThe Service Layer should not be coupled to HTTP/DRF.\nThink about the separation\n\n                 HTTP world\n                    ↓\n               APIView\n                    ↓\n              Serializer\n                    ↓\n              Service Layer\n                    ↓\n              Database\n\nThe service should be usable even when there is no HTTP request.\nFor example, later our Celery worker might call:\n\nTaskService.create_task(...)\n\nThere is no request and no HTTP Response there.\nSo this is bad inside a service:\n\nreturn Response(..., status=403)\nInstead: raise PermissionError(\"User cannot create task\")\n\nThen the API layer decides how to represent that error over HTTP.\n\nBut let's improve PermissionError\nPython's built-in PermissionError works, but in a real Django application we often create custom application exceptions.\nFor example:\n\nclass ProjectAccessDenied(Exception):\n    pass\n\nThen:\nif not user_can_access_project:\n    raise ProjectAccessDenied()\n\nThe API layer can translate it to:\nProjectAccessDenied\n        ↓\n403 Forbidden\n\nThis gives us a clean separation:\n\nService\n  ↓\n\"Business operation failed\"\n  ↓\nException\n  ↓\nAPIView / Exception Handler\n  ↓\nHTTP 403\n\nNow let's build the complete flow\nFor our task creation API:\n\nPOST /projects/10/tasks/\nThe flow becomes:\nClient\n  ↓\nAPIView\n  ↓\nAuthentication\n  ↓\nPermission\n  ↓\nSerializer\n  ↓\nTaskService\n  ↓\nBusiness Rules\n  ↓\ntransaction.atomic()\n  ↓\nTask + ActivityLog\n  ↓\nResponse\n\nAnd the responsibilities are:\n\n\n| Layer | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| APIView | HTTP request/response |\n| Authentication | Identify user |\n| Permission | Basic access control |\n| Serializer | Validate input |\n| Service | Business workflow |\n| Transaction | Keep related DB changes atomic |\n| ORM | Database operations |",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "63ItMdVxssaGyo91HegA7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6.188509097769611,
      "y": 54676.904265274046,
      "width": 11,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b65",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 65533157,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 2117548331,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790749697351,
      "created": 1790749696965,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
