      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "bC65bIn6W4YonyJwX3LSH",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -553.6630534022304,
      "y": 75227.17379652405,
      "width": 605,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1049639045,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 2012998309,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1791263389312,
      "created": 1791263387769,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Next Question — Sorting\nThe user now says:\n\"Show the newest projects first.\"\n\nWhat should the API do?\nA) Fetch all projects and sort them in the frontend\nB) Send the sorting requirement to the backend/database\nGET /projects/?ordering=-created_at\n\nC) Sort them using Celery\nD) Store projects in Redis and sort there\n👉 Your answer?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Next Question — Sorting\nThe user now says:\n\"Show the newest projects first.\"\n\nWhat should the API do?\nA) Fetch all projects and sort them in the frontend\nB) Send the sorting requirement to the backend/database\nGET /projects/?ordering=-created_at\n\nC) Sort them using Celery\nD) Store projects in Redis and sort there\n👉 Your answer?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "neV_P_FTAjf0o0EaIPzDx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -502.1864909022304,
      "y": 75554.89254652405,
      "width": 1044.1640625,
      "height": 650,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6r",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 541257765,
      "version": 76,
      "versionNonce": 1463678277,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1791263526729,
      "created": 1791263461567,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ Correct — B\nWe send the sorting requirement to the backend:\nGET /projects/?ordering=-created_at\n\nBackend/database handles it: Project.objects.order_by(\"-created_at\")\n\n- means descending, so newest projects come first.\n\nNow we can combine everything\nA realistic API could be:\nGET /projects/?status=ACTIVE&search=payment&ordering=-created_at&page=2&page_size=20\n\nThe flow is:\nRequest\n   ↓\nFilter: ACTIVE\n   ↓\nSearch: \"payment\"\n   ↓\nSort: newest first\n   ↓\nPagination: page 2, 20 records\n   ↓\nResponse\n\nThis is the typical pattern you'll see in production APIs.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ Correct — B\nWe send the sorting requirement to the backend:\nGET /projects/?ordering=-created_at\n\nBackend/database handles it: Project.objects.order_by(\"-created_at\")\n\n- means descending, so newest projects come first.\n\nNow we can combine everything\nA realistic API could be:\nGET /projects/?status=ACTIVE&search=payment&ordering=-created_at&page=2&page_size=20\n\nThe flow is:\nRequest\n   ↓\nFilter: ACTIVE\n   ↓\nSearch: \"payment\"\n   ↓\nSort: newest first\n   ↓\nPagination: page 2, 20 records\n   ↓\nResponse\n\nThis is the typical pattern you'll see in production APIs.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "zZ6gAxbRz7SYeJst5EZM_",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -580.6630534022304,
      "y": 76282.17379652405,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6s",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 60940133,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 778599813,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1791263540445,
      "created": 1791263539018,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Next Question — Important Production Concept\nImagine our Project table has 10 million records.\nWe frequently query:\nProject.objects.filter(status=\"ACTIVE\")\n\n\nWhat should we consider adding to the database to make this query\nfaster?\nA) Database index on status\nB) Store all projects in Python memory\nC) Use Celery\nD) Increase the API response size",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Next Question — Important Production Concept\nImagine our Project table has 10 million records.\nWe frequently query:\nProject.objects.filter(status=\"ACTIVE\")\n\n\nWhat should we consider adding to the database to make this query faster?\nA) Database index on status\nB) Store all projects in Python memory\nC) Use Celery\nD) Increase the API response size",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "6j34VjysxUZ5stGv8E2Bd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -530.5575846522304,
      "y": 76614.90426527405,
      "width": 1016.515625,
      "height": 975,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6t",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 736039685,
      "version": 167,
      "versionNonce": 832905515,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1791263701038,
      "created": 1791263619851,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ Correct — A) Database index on status\nAn index helps the database find matching rows faster instead of scanning the entire table.\nWithout an index:\n\n10 million projects\n       ↓\nCheck row 1\nCheck row 2\nCheck row 3\n...\nCheck row 10,000,000\n\nWith an index:\n             Index\n               ↓\n        status = ACTIVE\n               ↓\n       matching row locations\n               ↓\n          fetch rows\n\nBut one important interview nuance\nDon't say:\n\"Index always makes queries faster.\"\n\nBetter:\nAn index can significantly improve read queries that frequently filter, sort, or join on\nindexed columns, but indexes also consume storage and add overhead to INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE\noperations.\n\nFor example:\nclass Project(models.Model):\n    status = models.CharField(\n        max_length=20,\n        db_index=True\n    )\n\nHowever, for our real project, we should think about which queries are actually frequent and\nselective before adding indexes everywhere.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ Correct — A) Database index on status\nAn index helps the database find matching rows faster instead of scanning the entire table.\nWithout an index:\n\n10 million projects\n       ↓\nCheck row 1\nCheck row 2\nCheck row 3\n...\nCheck row 10,000,000\n\nWith an index:\n             Index\n               ↓\n        status = ACTIVE\n               ↓\n       matching row locations\n               ↓\n          fetch rows\n\nBut one important interview nuance\nDon't say:\n\"Index always makes queries faster.\"\n\nBetter:\nAn index can significantly improve read queries that frequently filter, sort, or join on indexed columns, but indexes also consume storage and add overhead to INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE operations.\n\nFor example:\nclass Project(models.Model):\n    status = models.CharField(\n        max_length=20,\n        db_index=True\n    )\n\nHowever, for our real project, we should think about which queries are actually frequent and selective before adding indexes everywhere.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "KUhjzBhrvVNfiAZBqNvgb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -623.6630534022304,
      "y": 77697.17379652405,
      "width": 1012.65234375,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6u",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1456573707,
      "version": 49,
      "versionNonce": 904780261,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1791263739148,
      "created": 1791263733633,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Next Question — Composite Index\nSuppose our most common API query is:\nGET /projects/?organization_id=10&status=ACTIVE\n\nAnd the database frequently executes:\nWHERE organization_id = 10\n  AND status = 'ACTIVE'\n\nWould you consider:\nA) Only an index on organization_id\nB) Only an index on status\nC) A composite index on (organization_id, status)\nD) No index because PostgreSQL handles everything automatically\n👉 Your answer?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Next Question — Composite Index\nSuppose our most common API query is:\nGET /projects/?organization_id=10&status=ACTIVE\n\nAnd the database frequently executes:\nWHERE organization_id = 10\n  AND status = 'ACTIVE'\n\nWould you consider:\nA) Only an index on organization_id\nB) Only an index on status\nC) A composite index on (organization_id, status)\nD) No index because PostgreSQL handles everything automatically\n👉 Your answer?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "lqYidcHTzJ-qstHsnKDf1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -573.3036784022304,
      "y": 78066.91598402405,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 1075,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6v",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 990414853,
      "version": 51,
      "versionNonce": 1059905611,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1791263882695,
      "created": 1791263761233,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ Correct — C) Composite index on (organization_id, status)\nBecause our common query uses both columns together:\nWHERE organization_id = 10\nAND status = 'ACTIVE'\n\nSo we can create:\nclass Meta:\n    indexes = [\n        models.Index(\n            fields=[\"organization_id\", \"status\"]\n        )\n    ]\n\nConceptually:\n\nComposite Index\n(organization_id, status)\n\n        ↓\n(10, ACTIVE)\n(10, COMPLETED)\n(10, INACTIVE)\n(11, ACTIVE)\n(11, COMPLETED)\n...\n\nThe database can efficiently locate:\norganization_id = 10\n        +\nstatus = ACTIVE\n\nImportant interview point\nThe order of columns matters in a composite index.\n\nFor: (organization_id, status)\n\nit's generally useful for queries involving:\norganization_id\norganization_id + status\n\nBut it may not be the best index for a query filtering only by status\nThis is why we design indexes based on our actual query patterns, not\njust based on which columns exist.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ Correct — C) Composite index on (organization_id, status)\nBecause our common query uses both columns together:\nWHERE organization_id = 10\nAND status = 'ACTIVE'\n\nSo we can create:\nclass Meta:\n    indexes = [\n        models.Index(\n            fields=[\"organization_id\", \"status\"]\n        )\n    ]\n\nConceptually:\n\nComposite Index\n(organization_id, status)\n\n        ↓\n(10, ACTIVE)\n(10, COMPLETED)\n(10, INACTIVE)\n(11, ACTIVE)\n(11, COMPLETED)\n...\n\nThe database can efficiently locate:\norganization_id = 10\n        +\nstatus = ACTIVE\n\nImportant interview point\nThe order of columns matters in a composite index.\n\nFor: (organization_id, status)\n\nit's generally useful for queries involving:\norganization_id\norganization_id + status\n\nBut it may not be the best index for a query filtering only by status\nThis is why we design indexes based on our actual query patterns, not just based on which columns exist.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "SOSgyM6SxvcpRPEBtLWeR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -638.7880534022304,
      "y": 79212.67379652405,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6w",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1081090091,
      "version": 5,
      "versionNonce": 1184339883,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1791264179247,
      "created": 1791263904190,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Next Question — Very important for our multi-tenant project\nOur application has:\nOrganization\n   ↓\nProject\n\nSuppose User A belongs to Organization 10, and User B belongs to\nOrganization 20.\nUser A sends:\nGET /projects/123/\n\nbut Project 123 belongs to Organization 20.\nWhat should our backend do?\nA) Return Project 123 because the user knows its ID\nB) Return 403 Forbidden\nC) Return 404 Not Found (so we don't reveal that a resource belonging to\nanother tenant exists)\nD) Return all projects from Organization 20\n👉 Choose the best answer.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Next Question — Very important for our multi-tenant project\nOur application has:\nOrganization\n   ↓\nProject\n\nSuppose User A belongs to Organization 10, and User B belongs to Organization 20.\nUser A sends:\nGET /projects/123/\n\nbut Project 123 belongs to Organization 20.\nWhat should our backend do?\nA) Return Project 123 because the user knows its ID\nB) Return 403 Forbidden\nC) Return 404 Not Found (so we don't reveal that a resource belonging to another tenant exists)\nD) Return all projects from Organization 20\n👉 Choose the best answer.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "PR6mTKUsmwPy7b7_Ii4Rc",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -621.1747721522304,
      "y": 79700.97457777405,
      "width": 1052.29296875,
      "height": 925,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6x",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 703139467,
      "version": 156,
      "versionNonce": 1815279141,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1791264251190,
      "created": 1791264174327,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ Correct — C is the best production choice here.\nThis is an important multi-tenant security pattern.\nIf User A belongs to Organization 10 and Project 123 belongs to Organization 20, we can\nintentionally return:\n404 Not Found\ninstead of: 403 Forbidden\n\nWhy?\nBecause 403 tells the user:\n\"Yes, Project 123 exists, but you aren't allowed to access it.\"\n\nThat can leak information about another organization's data.\nWith 404, we effectively say:\n\"This resource isn't available to you.\"\n\nConceptually, our query itself can enforce the tenant boundary:\nproject = Project.objects.get(\n    id=project_id,\n    organization__members__user=request.user\n)\n\nIf the project doesn't belong to one of the user's organizations:\nNo matching project\n      ↓\n404\n\nVery important distinction\nEarlier we said 403 for:\nAuthenticated user tries to access a resource they are forbidden from accessing.\n\nThat's still conceptually correct.\nBut for multi-tenant resource access, returning 404 instead of 403 is often a deliberate\nsecurity decision to prevent resource-existence leakage.\n\nSo in an interview, a strong answer is:\n\"Conceptually it's a 403 because the user is authenticated but unauthorized. However, for\ncross-tenant resources, I may return 404 to avoid revealing whether the resource exists.\"",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ Correct — C is the best production choice here.\nThis is an important multi-tenant security pattern.\nIf User A belongs to Organization 10 and Project 123 belongs to Organization 20, we can intentionally return:\n404 Not Found\ninstead of: 403 Forbidden\n\nWhy?\nBecause 403 tells the user:\n\"Yes, Project 123 exists, but you aren't allowed to access it.\"\n\nThat can leak information about another organization's data.\nWith 404, we effectively say:\n\"This resource isn't available to you.\"\n\nConceptually, our query itself can enforce the tenant boundary:\nproject = Project.objects.get(\n    id=project_id,\n    organization__members__user=request.user\n)\n\nIf the project doesn't belong to one of the user's organizations:\nNo matching project\n      ↓\n404\n\nVery important distinction\nEarlier we said 403 for:\nAuthenticated user tries to access a resource they are forbidden from accessing.\n\nThat's still conceptually correct.\nBut for multi-tenant resource access, returning 404 instead of 403 is often a deliberate security decision to prevent resource-existence leakage.\n\nSo in an interview, a strong answer is:\n\"Conceptually it's a 403 because the user is authenticated but unauthorized. However, for cross-tenant resources, I may return 404 to avoid revealing whether the resource exists.\"",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "acWajSyrXQJlFpvPJmK10",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -676.9325846522304,
      "y": 80689.90426527405,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1824001093,
      "version": 10,
      "versionNonce": 1820100325,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1791264286347,
      "created": 1791264270687,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Next Question — Query Optimization\nSuppose our project list API does this:\nprojects = Project.objects.filter(\n    organization=organization\n)\n\nfor project in projects:\n    print(project.team.name)\n\nThere are 1,000 projects.\nWhat database problem might we encounter because we're accessing\nproject.team inside the loop?\nA) N+1 query problem\nB) JWT expiration\nC) Race condition\nD) Deadlock\n👉 Your answer?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Next Question — Query Optimization\nSuppose our project list API does this:\nprojects = Project.objects.filter(\n    organization=organization\n)\n\nfor project in projects:\n    print(project.team.name)\n\nThere are 1,000 projects.\nWhat database problem might we encounter because we're accessing project.team inside the loop?\nA) N+1 query problem\nB) JWT expiration\nC) Race condition\nD) Deadlock\n👉 Your answer?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "yU1RzaoeE3FJ0oZ7SGeXc",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -634.4403971522304,
      "y": 81138.61520277405,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 1350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6z",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1813665029,
      "version": 63,
      "versionNonce": 1735964939,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1791264560289,
      "created": 1791264366550,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "✅ Correct — A) N+1 query problem\nThis is a very important Django ORM interview topic.\nSuppose:\n\nprojects = Project.objects.filter(\n    organization=organization\n)\n\nfor project in projects:\n    print(project.team.name)\n\nDjango may execute:\n1 query → fetch 1,000 projects\n\nThen:\n\n1 query → team for project 1\n1 query → team for project 2\n1 query → team for project 3\n...\n1 query → team for project 1,000\n\nSo approximately:\n1 + 1,000 = 1,001 queries 😱\n\nThat's the N+1 problem.\nHow do we fix it?\nSince Project → Team is a ForeignKey, use:\n\nprojects = Project.objects.filter(\n    organization=organization\n).select_related(\"team\")\n\nNow Django can fetch the projects and their related teams efficiently,\ntypically using a SQL JOIN:\n\nProjects ───── JOIN ───── Teams\n\nSo instead of:\n1001 queries\n\nwe can often get it down to: 1 query\nEasy rule to remember\n\nForeignKey / OneToOne\n        ↓\nselect_related()\n\nManyToMany / Reverse ForeignKey\n        ↓\nprefetch_related()\n\nWe'll go deeper into select_related vs prefetch_related later when we\ncover Django ORM optimization.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "✅ Correct — A) N+1 query problem\nThis is a very important Django ORM interview topic.\nSuppose:\n\nprojects = Project.objects.filter(\n    organization=organization\n)\n\nfor project in projects:\n    print(project.team.name)\n\nDjango may execute:\n1 query → fetch 1,000 projects\n\nThen:\n\n1 query → team for project 1\n1 query → team for project 2\n1 query → team for project 3\n...\n1 query → team for project 1,000\n\nSo approximately:\n1 + 1,000 = 1,001 queries 😱\n\nThat's the N+1 problem.\nHow do we fix it?\nSince Project → Team is a ForeignKey, use:\n\nprojects = Project.objects.filter(\n    organization=organization\n).select_related(\"team\")\n\nNow Django can fetch the projects and their related teams efficiently, typically using a SQL JOIN:\n\nProjects ───── JOIN ───── Teams\n\nSo instead of:\n1001 queries\n\nwe can often get it down to: 1 query\nEasy rule to remember\n\nForeignKey / OneToOne\n        ↓\nselect_related()\n\nManyToMany / Reverse ForeignKey\n        ↓\nprefetch_related()\n\nWe'll go deeper into select_related vs prefetch_related later when we cover Django ORM optimization.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "nzneOhXXGLKEIKI0xFAwk",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -692.6630534022304,
      "y": 82550.90426527405,
      "width": 11,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
